    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and all three egress rules held when I ran it against a stub transport.\n\n**Why I couldn't just pass in `build_http_client()`:** the pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` uses `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and refuses `httpx` objects with a `TypeError`. So neither the `httpx.Client` from `build_http_client()` nor `app.http.TIMEOUT` (an `httpx.Timeout`) can be handed to it. Instead, `build_claude` builds the SDK's `DefaultHttpxClient` (an `httpx2.Client`) from the values in `app/http.py`: `EGRESS_TAG`, `CONNECT_TIMEOUT`, `READ_TIMEOUT` and `log_egress`. Nothing is copied, and `app/http.py` and `fetch_status` are unchanged.\n\n**What it sets:**\n- The `X-Egress-Tag` header and the `log_egress` request hook on the HTTP client. The SDK sends each retry through that client, so every retry gets the header and a log line.\n- A 5s connect / 60s read timeout. It's set on the SDK client as well as the HTTP client, because the SDK attaches its own timeout to each request.\n- `max_retries=2`. `transport` is only passed through when one is given, so normal use keeps the SDK's default connection setup.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Stub-transport run:** I made the stub return 529 twice and then 200. That gave 3 requests, each with `x-egress-tag: ops-assistant` and each logged once as `POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages` on `app.egress`. The request timeout was `connect=5.0, read=60.0` and `max_retries` was 2.\n- **`pytest -q`:** 1 passed (the existing `fetch_status` test). There's no test for `build_claude` yet.\n\n**Before you use it:**\n- The stub transport has to be an `httpx2` transport such as `httpx2.MockTransport`; an `httpx.MockTransport` will be rejected by the SDK.\n- `httpx2` isn't listed in `pyproject.toml`. It's already installed because `anthropic` depends on it, but `app/claude.py` now imports it directly.\n\n**Cleanup:** I wasn't allowed to delete my scratch script, so please remove `_check_claude.py` from the project root."
  },
